 are often discarded early. But looking at history: across just played T1 and T4 recently, so both might be safe. However, T1 is more likely to be dangerous because someone might need it for terminal chow. But many terminals are already out. I'll check: T1 unshown 2 left, T4 unshown 3 left. Both have some left. I'll discard T1 because it's a terminal and less useful for my hand since I don't have other T1 or T2. Also, by discarding T1, I keep T4 which could pair with T5 or form chow with T3-T4-T5 or T4-T5-T6. But T5 and T6 are limited. Alternatively, discard T4. But I think T1 is slightly more useless because it's terminal and I have no other terminals in hand except W1 already used in chow. So discard T1.\\n\\nReason: My hand currently has two melds and needs two more melds and a pair. Keeping W5 and W6 offers the best chance to complete a chow with W7 (3 left). T1 and T4 are less useful due to scarce connecting tiles. Discarding T1 is relatively safe as it was recently discarded by another player and is a terminal, reducing its danger. Keeping B5 pair and W8 pair provides flexibility for the final pair. This move maintains tile efficiency and aims for a simple winning hand with potential patterns like Chicken Hand if no other patterns emerge.\\n\\nAnswer: Play T1\"}"}
